      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To screen and identify the genes of activated memory CD(4)(+) T cells in asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Differential display polymerase chain reaction (DDPCR) was utilized to identify genes of memory CD(4)(+) T cells after activation from asthmatic patients and normal individuals, and the mRNA levels of the differentially expressed genes were analyzed by reverse transcription-polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Nineteen differentially expressed cDNA fragments were isolated. The homology analysis indicated that two fragments possessed a high degree of homology with interleukin-7 (IL-7) mRNA and MM-1 mRNA. RT-PCR confirmed that IL-7 mRNA and MM-1 mRNA levels were increased in memory CD(4)(+) T cells after activation in asthmatic patients (0.390 +/- 0.029, 0.629 +/- 0.047, F = 983, 1264 respectively, all P < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The up-regulated expression of IL-7 and MM-1 genes in memory CD(4)(+) T cells after activation may be a molecular mechanism that differentiates the response to allergens of asthma patients from normal individuals."
        }
      ]},
